--- body ---
## Context
The author rejected R26 as written on 2026-08-29. The requirement asserted
that at every poll the indexed set is a most-recent-first prefix — "the
newest N items, never a gap in the middle". Three grounds, recorded in
DECISIONS.md: recency orients the work rather than imposing a verifiable
total order; items enter and leave the library in real time, so an invariant
over a positional prefix is asserted over a set that has already moved; and
a band partition of a moving set is not a stable thing to assert.

What replaces it is priority by content type. An item's title, keywords,
abstract and notes are embedded before its body content. That is checkable
at any instant without a snapshot of the library, which is what the prefix
observable was reaching for and could not have.

This is not a rewording. R26 changes what it promises, the convergence
harness changes what it asserts, and SPEC.md §5.2.3 loses the justification
for the machinery it describes.

## The open question this ticket must answer
The two-band frontier did anti-monopoly work. Band 0 carried each item's
first K passages on the newest-first frontier; band 1 drained behind it, so
one monster could not monopolize the pipeline. Tier priority replaces part
of that: records all precede body content, so a monster can no longer delay
every other item's *record*. It does not replace the rest — once the body
tier is reached, a 1 849-entry dictionary can still monopolize it.

Whether that needs a mechanism, and which, is open and is the substance of
this ticket. Options to weigh, not a menu to pick from blind: per-item
round-robin within the body tier; a per-item passage quantum; nothing at all
if the tier ordering plus honest partial-coverage reporting is judged
sufficient. Each has a different cost in ledger complexity and a different
failure mode under real-time item churn — which is the constraint that
killed the band approach and must not kill its replacement the same way.

## Relevant files
- `SPEC.md` §3 — R26; check R2 and R4 for consequential wording
- `SPEC.md` §5.2.3 — the two-band frontier, band 0/band 1, the derived cap K,
  and the "R26 observable is then asserted per phase" paragraph
- `SPEC.md` §5.2.8 — the convergence gate asserts prefix-ness today
- `SPEC.md` §4 — C2's smallest-first comparison rests on the same
  standard and must stay coherent
- `DECISIONS.md` — the ruling; do not edit the entry

## Actions
1. Rewrite R26: state recency as an orientation, state the tier priority as
   the checkable promise, drop the prefix observable.
2. Decide the intra-body-tier monopoly question above, with the reasoning
   recorded. If it needs a new rule, that rule goes to DECISIONS.md first.
3. Rework SPEC.md §5.2.3 to match. The derived cap K exists to serve band 0;
   if bands go, say what if anything K becomes.
4. Change the convergence harness to assert the tier ordering instead of
   prefix-ness.
5. Re-read C2's smallest-first paragraph: the rejection of #6012's ordering
   was argued on holding both designs to one standard, and that standard has
   changed.

## Test
The convergence harness is the test and it is currently asserting the wrong
thing. Write the tier-ordering assertion first and watch it fail against an
index built body-text-first — a positive control, because an assertion that
has never failed has not been shown to be able to.

## Verification
- [ ] R26's new text promises only what can be checked without a library
      snapshot
- [ ] No orphan machinery left in §2.3 — anything that existed only to serve
      the band observable is gone or re-justified
- [ ] The C2 smallest-first comparison still holds under the new standard
- [ ] `make check` green, figure-guard anchors updated

## Invariants
- The 2026-08-26 record-tier ruling stands, as amended 2026-08-29 to place
  notes in the record tier.
- R2 (most recent first) is not repealed — it is recency as orientation,
  which is what it always meant.
- Partial coverage stays honestly reported (R4, R17); losing the prefix
  observable must not become a reason to report less.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] R26 rewritten and consistent with R2 and R4
- [ ] The intra-body-tier monopoly question answered, ratified if it needs a
      rule
- [ ] SPEC.md §5.2.3 reworked with no orphan machinery
- [ ] The convergence gate asserts the tier ordering
- [ ] `make check` passes
